  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Among the people arrested in a certain city in the mid-1990s, $18%$ were males under $18$ years old and $54%$ were males of $18$ years old and over. What is the probability that a person selected at random from all those arrested is a male?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $M$ be the event that the chosen person is a male, and let $U$ be the event that the chosen person is under $18.$ Then, ${U}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the person is $18$ or over.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person is a male, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(M)=P(M\\cap U)+P(M\\cap {U}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nP(M\\cap U)=0.18,\\,P(M\\cap {U}^{\u2032})=0.54.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person is a male is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M) & =P(M\\cap U)+P(M\\cap {U}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =0.18+0.54 \\\\  & =0.72.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
